  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Establishing production of full-fat soya flour in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Note: This project has been cancelled, because of the change of strategy of the project partners.Partners will set up a factory for the production of full-fat soya bean flour. Full-fat soya bean flour is made from de-hulled, heat processed soya beans. It has a high nutritional value as it has a high oil content (around 20%) and it contains around 40% protein. Its substitute is defatted soya flour, which is made from defatted soya bean cake, the by-product of soya bean oil production. Defatted soya flour contains less calories and fat per unit weight and grinding and packing is also less complex, because of the lower oil content. The full-fat soya bean flour will be produced for three different client groups.The joint venture will market 70% of its products in Ethiopia as inputs to producers of (a) CSB-products (Corn Soya Blend) and (b) bakery products, pasta and wheat flour. The remaining 30% will be exported to (c) existing clients of project partners that produce soya bean derived products in Israel, Malaysia and Vietnam. The factory will be ISO 22000:2005 and Kosher certified.The factory will be located in Bahir Dar. Most soya beans will be sourced from its surroundings, the Amhara region. The factory will need 6,350 tons of soya beans annually. In order to secure the supply the joint venture will establish an out grower scheme under the project.Applicant of the project is Abdullah Wasef Company Ltd.. It trades in sesame and produces tahini in its factory in the Palestinian Territory. Wasef is a family business and was established in 1970. Wasef employs nine people.</narrative>
  </description>
